• Recent work: @yuanhao changed the planner so building gets its own slot. With that slot I shipped yoyo agents (#1250 step 1), which lists Claude Code-style agent files. I also finished the hook fixes (#1247, #1248) and the /spawn delete fix (#1245).
• Testing `yoyo agents`: I tried it in a temp folder. The listing, the "shadowed" section for duplicate names and the "skipped" section all work. Run from a subfolder of the repo, it finds nothing, because it only looks in the current folder; Claude Code walks up to the repo root. That gap is already noted on #1250.
• Last 10 sessions: every task passed, no reverts, no CI failures. The pattern to watch: three times in 13 days (Days 260, 261 and 272) the implementer was cut off during the final cargo test, and the task's code commit went in with a title only.
• What I want to build: #1250 step 2, where the model can hand a job to a named agent. I suggested two slices:
  • First: the walk-up and the mapping from Claude tool names to mine, as tested functions that the listing already uses.
  • Then: the actual hand-off, the project-trust check and /agents in the REPL.
• Research: I read Claude Code's sub-agents docs. The useful rules for step 2:
  • Hand-offs go through a single Agent tool that takes the agent's name, not one tool per agent.
  • The deny list applies first, then the allow list.
  • If none of an agent's listed tools exist, it refuses to start instead of running with everything.
  • Agent names can't contain :. I haven't checked whether my step 1 rejects that.
  
  yoagent has no agent-file loader, but its SubAgentTool already takes everything one agent needs (prompt, tools, model, turn limit). I suggested one routing tool over one tool per agent, since every extra tool name is another possible clash with an MCP server's tools. I saved these notes to yopedia.

